  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Keyboard Focus**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how to make all components genuinely support remote keyboard control. My initial observation is that sub-components lack inherent keyboard focus handlers, relying more on click handlers. I\'m brainstorming the best way to implement a universal remote keyboard control system that encompasses every component, and the challenges I\'ll face.\n\n\n**Developing Remote Interaction System**\n\nI\'m now thinking about how to build a universal remote control engine. Drawing inspiration from TV app design, I\'ve got an idea: we could track the focusable item\'s DOM reference. The `tabIndex` attribute seems key here. I will ensure every interactive element uses `data-remote`, or is standard focusable, to work with arrow keys. When `remoteMode` is active, I\'m thinking of using browser focus management, specifically classifying focusable elements into a \"Sidebar Navigation Zone\" and a \"Main Dashboard Zone.\"\n\n\n**Refining Zone Navigation**\n\nI\'m now refining the focus management logic for the main dashboard zone and the sidebar. My plan is to classify elements as belonging to either the \"Sidebar Navigation Zone\" or the \"Main Dashboard Zone.\" The `ArrowDown` and `ArrowUp` keys will navigate the sidebar, while `ArrowRight` will shift focus to the main content. The main challenge is defining the focus navigation within the main dashboard\342\200\224what will be the best way to handle this?\n\n\n**Designing Spatial Navigation**\n\nMy plan is to implement spatial navigation, either purely or as a hybrid approach with sequential navigation. I\'m leaning toward a grid-based engine within `App.tsx` and focusing on the logic for spatial matching. My initial step involves querying all focusable elements in the `<main>` container and obtaining their bounding client rects. I will then devise specific logic for `ArrowDown` and `ArrowUp` to locate the closest elements based on vertical positioning relative to the currently focused element. The system will prioritize the closest vertical distance.\n\n\n**Implementing Spatial Navigation**\n\nI\'m now refining the spatial navigation, inspired by Smart TV browsers. For `ArrowUp`, I\'ll find elements directly above, prioritizing the closest. For `ArrowDown`, the same, but below. `ArrowRight` will target elements to the right, minimizing horizontal distance, and `ArrowLeft` will go left, or back to the sidebar if at the leftmost boundary. This grid-based approach feels very robust, completely automatic, and dynamically adapts. It\'s brilliant.\n\n\n**Expanding Spatial Navigation**\n\nI\'m now thinking about comprehensive layout coverage! I\'ve written some TypeScript to calculate distances between elements. To get optimal navigation, my distance function penalizes misaligned elements in the primary navigation direction, making it simple to find the next element by prioritizing alignment and vertical/horizontal distance. I\'m keen to ensure perfect coverage of the application.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will view `/src/index.css` to see the global styles so we can inject a beautiful, globally-supported, remote-friendly focus glow for focused elements."
